# frontend/

The SvelteKit application for `{{.AppName}}`. It is an ordinary pnpm
project: no Go files live here, and nothing in it is Nise-specific except
where this page says otherwise.

```sh
pnpm install     # once
pnpm dev         # Vite dev server, hot reload
pnpm build       # production build — see below
pnpm check       # stale models, runes-only lint, then svelte-check
pnpm test:unit   # typed API-client unit contracts
```

## OpenAPI models and client

`../api/openapi.yaml` is authoritative. The frontend pins
openapi-typescript and checks its complete output into
`src/lib/api/schema.d.ts`:

```sh
pnpm api:generate  # replace schema.d.ts
pnpm api:check     # non-mutating stale-output check
```

Do not edit `schema.d.ts`. `src/lib/api/client.ts` is application-owned: it
uses the generated path/operation types, always sends same-origin browser
credentials, forwards `AbortSignal`, and maps HTTP and transport failures to
stable error classes without copying response details into their messages.
Extend that wrapper deliberately as the API gains real operations; do not add
a second client framework beside it.

Successful single-resource calls resolve to the generated resource directly.
Generated collection models have required `items` and `page` members; treat
empty `items` as `[]`, never `null`, and do not add a client-side envelope.

`pnpm build` and `pnpm check` both refuse stale models. The initial Vitest
suite is Node-only and covers the fetch wrapper. Browser component and
Playwright coverage arrive with the application-shell milestone.

## Runes only

This application is Svelte 5 runes-only, and `pnpm check` refuses Svelte 4
syntax:

| Svelte 4 | Use instead |
|---|---|
| `$: value = …` | `$derived` for a value, `$effect` for a side effect |
| `export let prop` | destructure from `$props()` |
| `$$props`, `$$restProps`, `$$slots` | take the rest of `$props()` explicitly; snippets replace slots |
| `createEventDispatcher` | a callback prop, such as `onselect` |
| `beforeUpdate` / `afterUpdate` | `$effect`, or `$effect.pre` |
| `import { writable } from 'svelte/store'` | a `.svelte.ts` module holding `$state` |
| `on:click={…}` | `onclick={…}` |
| `<slot />`, `let:item` | a snippet prop and `{@render …}` |

Svelte 5 accepts every construct on the left, which is why this is checked
rather than assumed: none of them is a compile error or a `svelte-check`
finding. Shared reactive state belongs in a `.svelte.ts` module holding
`$state`, not in a store.

The checker is `scripts/check-runes.mjs` — one owned file, no dependency,
run by `pnpm check:runes` and by `pnpm check`. It reads `<script>` blocks
and markup separately, so a Tailwind `md:` class, a `$:` inside a string,
and an `on:` inside a comment are not findings. Edit its rules, add to them,
or delete the file; prefer editing over leaving a violation unreported.

## `pnpm build` writes outside this directory

This is the one surprise, and it is deliberate:

```
frontend/  →  ../internal/platform/webui/embedded/client/
```

`go:embed` cannot reach outside the directory of the package that declares
it, so the built application has to land inside the Go package that embeds
it. Pointing `adapter-static` at that directory is how this project keeps
`frontend/` free of Go files and `internal/platform/webui/` free of frontend
sources. The output path is set in `svelte.config.js`.

The adapter **deletes its output directory before every build**. Nothing may
be committed inside `embedded/client/`; the committed placeholder that keeps
`go build` working in a fresh clone is its sibling,
`embedded/placeholder.html`.

`make dist` is the shipping build: it runs `pnpm build` and then compiles the
binary around its output. Plain `make build` deliberately does not, so a Go
change can be compiled without Node installed — it embeds whatever build is
already there, or the placeholder page when there is none. `make web-clean`
removes the build and returns to the placeholder.

## One HTML document, and only one

`src/routes/+layout.ts` sets `ssr = false` and `prerender = false` for the
whole tree, so the build produces exactly one HTML document: the
`fallback: 'index.html'` page named in `svelte.config.js`. The Go server
serves that document for every path it does not recognise as an asset, and
client-side routing takes it from there.

Prerendering is off rather than on because there is nothing to prerender:
with `ssr = false` every route renders to the same empty shell, so
prerendering would write one identical copy per route and the fallback would
then overwrite `index.html` at the end of the build. It also states the
security rule structurally — a protected route is never written to disk at
build time. A genuinely public, content-shaped page may set
`export const prerender = true` in its own `+page.ts`, but only if it needs
no session: a prerendered page is written once at build time and served
identically to everyone.

The Go server reads that document's inline bootstrap script at startup and
puts its hash into the Content Security Policy
(`internal/platform/webui/webui.go`). A build that produced no inline script
fails closed rather than serving a page the policy would blank.

## Two settings that must stay at their defaults

- **`kit.csp` is unset.** The Go server sends the single authoritative
  Content Security Policy as a response header. SvelteKit would emit a
  second one as a `<meta http-equiv>` tag for prerendered output, where
  `frame-ancestors` and `report-uri` are ignored and the two policies apply
  as their intersection.
- **`kit.inlineStyleThreshold` is 0.** Raising it inlines stylesheets into
  the page, which `style-src 'self'` blocks.

## No inline `style` attribute

The policy has no `style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'`. Use a class or Svelte's
`style:` directive, which compiles to CSSOM property writes that CSP does
not govern. Avoid `<Component --token="value" />` too: it wraps the
component in an element carrying an inline `style` attribute. Set the custom
property from a stylesheet rule instead.

`app.css` restores the geometry of SvelteKit's own accessibility announcer,
whose inline style attribute the policy blocks. Do not remove that rule.

## What is deliberately absent

This is a minimal but real SvelteKit project: it installs, builds,
type-checks, tests its API client, and renders one page served by the Go
binary. The application shell — navigation, theme and language controls,
authentication screens, tables, and the component set — arrives with a later
milestone, as do the Vitest browser-component suite and the Playwright
end-to-end suite. This tree is where all of it lands.
